Image Segmentation (Medical X-ray Image Segmentation)
Image segmentation is a fundamental computer vision technique that partitions an image into meaningful
regions by assigning a class label to each pixel or group of pixels, enabling precise localization and
delineation of objects or structures of interest. Conventional segmentation is broadly categorized into
semantic segmentation (assigning a class label to every pixel without distinguishing object instances),
instance segmentation (separating individual object instances of the same class), and panoptic segmen-
tation (jointly performing semantic and instance segmentation). In medical imaging, segmentation of
X-ray images is essential for identifying anatomical structures such as bones, localizing fracture regions,
and separating them from the background and other tissues, thereby supporting computer-aided diagno-
sis, treatment planning, and clinical decision-making. Conventional segmentation approaches include
classical techniques (thresholding, edge detection, region growing, watershed, and active contours),
convolutional encoder–decoder architectures (FCN, U-Net, U-Net++, Attention U-Net, DeepLabV3+),
self-configuring frameworks (nnU-Net), transformer-based models (TransUNet, Swin-UNet, UNETR,
SegFormer, Mask2Former), state-space (Mamba-based) models (U-Mamba, VM-UNet, Swin-UMamba),
diffusion-based segmentation models (MedSegDiff), and promptable foundation models (Segment Any-
thing Model (SAM), SAM 2, MedSAM). However, existing methods face several challenges, including
low contrast and overlapping anatomical structures in X-ray images, thin and irregular fracture lines,
severe class imbalance between background, bone, and fracture pixels, limited availability of annotated
medical data, imprecise boundary delineation, poor generalization across scanners and patient popula-
tions, high computational cost of large foundation and transformer models, and the domain gap between
natural-image and medical-image pre-training.
In this research, the different types of image segmentation will first be studied, and the latest image
segmentation models and techniques will be surveyed and compared through a structured literature review.
Based on their architectural characteristics, accuracy, boundary-preservation capability, data efficiency,
and computational requirements, the models most suitable for segmenting X-ray images into bone, fracture
region, background, and any additional relevant classes (e.g., soft tissue or implants) will be identified
and justified. The working principle of the selected baseline models will then be demonstrated using
publicly available X-ray datasets (e.g., FracAtlas, GRAZPEDWRI-DX, publicly available bone fracture
X-ray segmentation datasets, or equivalent open-access collections) after appropriate preprocessing,
including contrast enhancement (e.g., CLAHE), resizing, intensity normalization, and data augmentation.
Subsequently, potential improvements will be investigated through hybrid CNN–Transformer or CNN–
Mamba architectures, attention and multi-scale feature fusion, boundary-aware and topology-aware
refinement, adaptive and class-imbalance-aware loss functions (e.g., Dice, Focal, Tversky, and boundary
losses), parameter-efficient fine-tuning of foundation models (e.g., adapter- or LoRA-based MedSAM),
prompt-guided segmentation, and uncertainty-aware prediction. The proposed modifications should be
supported by mathematical formulations, theoretical analysis or derivations wherever applicable, ablation
studies, and experimental validation. The effectiveness of the proposed model will be evaluated against
conventional baseline models (e.g., U-Net, U-Net++, DeepLabV3+, TransUNet, and nnU-Net) in terms
of Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC), Intersection over Union (IoU), precision, recall, pixel accuracy,
Hausdorff distance, class-wise performance (especially for the fracture class), model size, parameter
count, FLOPs, inference latency, robustness, and generalization.
Problem Statement:1
To investigate the types, working principles, and limitations of conventional and state-of-the-art image
segmentation models, identify the models most suitable for multi-class segmentation of X-ray images
(bone, fracture region, background, and other relevant classes), and develop an enhanced segmentation
framework through novel mathematical or algorithmic modifications using publicly available X-ray
